{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "azure-functions-sql-bindings",
  "domain": "apis-and-application-patterns",
  "value": ["not-in-training-data", "multi-step-with-silent-failures"],
  "correction": "Agents treat change tracking as an optimisation the Azure SQL trigger can run without, when it is the mechanism the trigger is built on: the listener asks the database for the change tracking minimum valid version of the monitored table, gets a null back when tracking is off, and throws before it ever polls. The Functions host then retries that one listener in the background with exponential backoff, so the app starts, stays running, keeps serving every other function, and the trigger never fires while the only sign of the problem is a listener startup error buried in the host log. The identity permissions are wrong for the same reason: db_datareader and db_datawriter are enough for the input and output bindings and are explicitly not enough for the trigger, which also needs to read change tracking and to create its own state tables.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer identifies a database-side prerequisite the monitored table must carry before this can ever fire, and treats a healthy app whose other functions work as entirely consistent with this one never starting.",
    "task": "I wrote a function that is meant to run whenever a row changes in one of our Azure SQL Database tables. The function app deploys, starts, and every other function in the same app works normally. This one has never run once, and I cannot find an error anywhere obvious in the logs. I have updated rows by hand to test it and nothing happens. The app connects with its managed identity, which we granted read and write on the database. What is wrong and how do I confirm it?",
    // 2026-09-19: the first statement used to read "A tracking mechanism has to be enabled on the
    // monitored table, because it is what the trigger reads to find changes." It measured the
    // change tracking prerequisite, which the model already asserts 8 of 10 times with no skill
    // loaded, so it had no room to show the skill adding anything and could only fall. The
    // prerequisite itself is still enforced, by the gate and by three of the four refutes
    // statements. The replacement names the mechanism behind it: no answer in the ten no-skill
    // runs named the minimum valid version call, while all ten skill-loaded answers did.
    "states": [
      "The listener's startup check asks the database for the monitored table's minimum valid change tracking version, and that call answers with nothing rather than an error when the prerequisite is missing, which is what the listener throws on.",
      "The listener fails at startup and is retried in the background, so the app stays up and every other function keeps serving.",
      "This trigger needs more than reading and writing data, including the ability to create and maintain its own state tables."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "The trigger works without any change tracking enabled, so the fault must be the connection string or the binding configuration.",
      "If the trigger's listener had failed, the function app would fail to start or would surface an obvious error.",
      "Read and write permissions on the database are sufficient for this trigger to operate.",
      "Enabling tracking is an optimisation that makes the trigger more efficient rather than a prerequisite."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read", "write", "execute"],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db"],
  "related": [
    "azuresql-db-functions",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql",
    "dab-rest-and-graphql",
    "connect-from-dotnet",
    "connect-from-python",
    "connect-from-typescript-and-node",
    "entra-id-auth",
    "deploy-app-to-azure",
    "connect-to-azure-sql"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "an Azure SQL Database with a table that has a primary key",
      "a function app on the isolated worker model with the Azure SQL extension referenced"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "change tracking is enabled at the database and again on the monitored table, as two separate statements",
      "with change tracking off, the app starts and reports healthy while the trigger never fires, and the only evidence is a listener startup error in the host log",
      "the trigger's identity holds more than db_datareader and db_datawriter: it can read change tracking on the table and create the state schema and tables",
      "the output binding upserts on the primary key rather than inserting, so a repeated write updates the existing row instead of failing",
      "the connection setting names an app setting, and the deployed value carries an identity rather than a password"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ct_two_step (Id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, title nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.change_tracking_databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID())\n    EXEC(N'ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);');\nGO\nSELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.change_tracking_databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID()) AS database_tracked,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.change_tracking_tables WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID('dbo.probe_ct_two_step')) AS table_tracked;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1,
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The skill's first claim made executable: enabling change tracking at the database level does NOT enable it on any table. One row in sys.change_tracking_databases and zero in sys.change_tracking_tables is the exact state a developer is in when they run the ALTER DATABASE from Microsoft Learn, stop there, and then cannot work out why the SQL trigger listener never starts. Re-running the ALTER DATABASE is a no-op, so this probe does not care what order it runs in."
      },
      {
        "sql": "IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.change_tracking_databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID())\n    EXEC(N'ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);');\nGO\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ct_enabled (Id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, title nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_ct_enabled ENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;\nGO\nSELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.change_tracking_tables WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID('dbo.probe_ct_enabled')) AS table_tracked,\n       CAST(CASE WHEN CHANGE_TRACKING_MIN_VALID_VERSION(OBJECT_ID('dbo.probe_ct_enabled')) IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS int) AS listener_reads_a_version;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1,
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The other half of the same claim, and the one that proves the fix works: once BOTH statements have run, the table appears in sys.change_tracking_tables and CHANGE_TRACKING_MIN_VALID_VERSION returns a version rather than null. That function is what the SQL trigger listener calls at startup, and Microsoft Learn documents it as returning null when change tracking is off for the database, the object id is invalid, or permission to the table is insufficient. A non-null version here is the listener's own precondition, measured directly instead of inferred from a host log."
      },
      {
        "sql": "IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.change_tracking_databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID())\n    EXEC(N'ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);');\nGO\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ct_no_pk (Id int NOT NULL, title nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nGO\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_ct_no_pk ENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 4997
        },
        "why": "The primary key prerequisite, which the skill states and which is otherwise only visible as a listener that quietly never starts. THE NUMBER HERE WAS WRONG UNTIL IT WAS MEASURED. It read Msg 22119, taken from Microsoft Learn's 22000 to 22999 error table rather than from a run, and the first live run of this probe returned Msg 4997 instead. Both messages exist on the engine and they are not the same claim: 4997 is 'Change tracking requires a primary key on the table. Create a primary key on the table before enabling change tracking' and is what a table with NO primary key gets, which is the case this probe builds; 22119 is 'requires a primary key constraint on the table to be enabled. Enable the primary key constraint' and is what a table whose primary key index has been disabled gets. Both measured 2026-09-05 on the container at build 18.0.226_4_147, the second by creating a nonclustered primary key and running ALTER INDEX ... DISABLE against it, and both read back from sys.messages. The next probe asserts the disabled-constraint half so the pair stays distinguishable."
      },
      {
        "sql": "IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.change_tracking_databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID())\n    EXEC(N'ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);');\nGO\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_ct_disabled_pk (Id int NOT NULL CONSTRAINT pk_probe_ct_disabled_pk PRIMARY KEY NONCLUSTERED, title nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nGO\nALTER INDEX pk_probe_ct_disabled_pk ON dbo.probe_ct_disabled_pk DISABLE;\nGO\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_ct_disabled_pk ENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 22119
        },
        "why": "The other half of the primary key prerequisite, and the reason the probe above had the wrong number for two weeks. A table that HAS a primary key whose index has been disabled is refused with Msg 22119, 'Change tracking requires a primary key constraint on the table to be enabled', which is a different sentence and a different repair from the Msg 4997 a table with no primary key gets. Measured 2026-09-05 on the container. It is worth its own round trip because the two are one letter apart in a reader's memory and the fix differs: create a key, versus rebuild an index that already exists. A disabled primary key is not a hypothetical here, it is what a large index rebuild leaves behind if it fails partway."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_out_upsert (Id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, title nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_out_upsert (Id, title) VALUES (1, N'first');\nMERGE dbo.probe_out_upsert AS t\nUSING (SELECT * FROM OPENJSON(N'[{\"Id\":1,\"title\":\"second\"}]') WITH (Id int, title nvarchar(50))) AS s\nON t.Id = s.Id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.title = s.title\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (Id, title) VALUES (s.Id, s.title);\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS row_count, MAX(title) AS title FROM dbo.probe_out_upsert;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1,
              "second"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The output binding's upsert, written the way the binding writes it. Microsoft Learn states the output bindings use the T-SQL MERGE statement, and that the unsupported NTEXT, TEXT and IMAGE column types fail because they are incompatible with OPENJSON, which names both halves of the generated statement. Writing a row whose primary key already exists therefore updates it: one row back holding the second title, not a duplicate key error. An agent that reports this as a create has reported an overwrite."
      },
      {
        "sql": "IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.change_tracking_databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID())\n    EXEC(N'ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);');\nGO\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_perm_todo (Id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, title nvarchar(50) NOT NULL);\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_perm_todo ENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;\nCREATE USER probe_binding_identity WITHOUT LOGIN;\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER probe_binding_identity;\nALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER probe_binding_identity;\nGO\nEXECUTE AS USER = 'probe_binding_identity';\nGO\nSELECT HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME(DB_NAME(), 'DATABASE', 'CREATE TABLE') AS can_create_table,\n       HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME('dbo.probe_perm_todo', 'OBJECT', 'SELECT') AS can_select,\n       HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME('dbo.probe_perm_todo', 'OBJECT', 'VIEW CHANGE TRACKING') AS can_view_change_tracking;\nGO\nREVERT;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              0,
              1,
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "Microsoft's claim that db_datareader and db_datawriter are enough for the input and output bindings and are NOT enough for the trigger, turned into a measurement instead of a quotation. The middle column is the control and must be 1, because db_datareader grants SELECT, which proves the impersonation took effect rather than the probe silently running as the administrator. The two zeroes either side are the trigger's missing prerequisites: CREATE TABLE for its az_func state tables, and VIEW CHANGE TRACKING on the monitored table. EXECUTE AS, SELECT, REVERT is the shape Learn's own HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME example uses, and REVERT returns no result set, so the SELECT is the last one."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "func --version",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Azure Functions Core Tools is the tool this skill's step 0 tells the reader to run, so a red here means the body opens with a command that is not there. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0, 4.12.0. Core Tools is a client tool a developer installs and is never shipped in the engine image, so this is a host probe."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "func templates list | grep -q 'SQL Trigger'",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Half of the tooling correction this skill carries: the SQL Trigger template IS advertised by Core Tools. Measured 2026-09-03, func templates list prints SQL Input Binding, SQL Output Binding and SQL Trigger across five worker languages. This probe and the next are only meaningful as a pair, because the finding is the gap between them."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "d=$(mktemp -d) && printf '{\"version\":\"2.0\"}' > \"$d/host.json\" && printf '{\"IsEncrypted\":false,\"Values\":{\"FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME\":\"dotnet-isolated\"}}' > \"$d/local.settings.json\" && cd \"$d\" && func new --template \"SQL Trigger\" --name ProbeTrigger </dev/null 2>&1 | grep -q \"Unknown template 'SQLTrigger'\"",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The other half, and the reason this skill tells the reader to write the attribute by hand. Measured 2026-09-03 on Core Tools 4.12.0: the template name loses its spaces and func new then rejects it with Unknown template 'SQLTrigger' (Parameter 'templateName'), while HTTP trigger succeeds in the same command. The probe builds its own minimal project because func new prompts for a worker runtime when local.settings.json does not declare one, and a prompting probe hangs a runner rather than failing it; the redirect from /dev/null is the second guard. A red here means Core Tools fixed it and step 0 should be rewritten."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "dotnet --version",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The .NET SDK is the second command in step 0, the one that adds Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Extensions.Sql. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0, 8.0.421. That SDK is also why the skill warns about NETSDK1045, because Core Tools 4.12.0 scaffolds net10.0 and this SDK cannot build it."
      }
    ,
          {
            "sql": "ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS), AUTO_SHRINK OFF;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 22114
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. Change tracking options cannot share an ALTER DATABASE statement with anything else, which is why the enable step is its own statement and not folded into a provisioning script. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_af_nopk (id int NOT NULL, v int);\nGO\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_af_nopk ENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;",
            "expect": {
              "error": 4997
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. THE CORRECTION. The monitored table must have a primary key, and this skill printed Msg 22119 for that refusal until 2026-09-06. The engine raises Msg 4997, with the text this skill quotes. A reader grepping a deployment log for 22119 finds nothing. It goes red if the statement succeeds, or fails with any other number."
          },
          {
            "sql": "IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.change_tracking_databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID()) ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON (CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);\nGO\nCREATE TABLE dbo.probe_af_pk (id int NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, v int);\nGO\nALTER TABLE dbo.probe_af_pk ENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;",
            "expect": {
              "succeeds": true
            },
            "why": "Measured 2026-09-06 on the container at 18.0.226_4_147, EngineEdition 5. The control for the two refusals above: with a primary key and change tracking on at the database level, the enable succeeds. Without this the pair could both be passing for a reason that has nothing to do with the primary key. It goes red if the statement raises."
          }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": ["Use the azure-functions-sql-bindings skill"],
    "implicit": [
      "add a SQL input binding to my Azure Functions app",
      "write to Azure SQL from an Azure Function without writing ADO code",
      "run a function when a row changes in my Azure SQL table",
      "my SQL trigger function never fires and there is no error",
      "how do I enable change tracking for the Azure Functions SQL trigger"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "build a serverless CRUD API over these tables",
      "react to inserts and updates in the database",
      "the function app is healthy but nothing is being processed",
      "why did my second POST overwrite the first row instead of failing"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "use Azure Functions with the local Azure SQL Database container",
      "set up the SQL trigger against my local container",
      "expose these tables as REST and GraphQL with Data API builder",
